//   Courses:
//   - MIT 18.212 Algebraic Combinatorics (Postnikov), https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-212-algebraic-combinatorics-spring-2019/pages/syllabus/
//   - MIT 18.211 Combinatorial Analysis (Zhao, 2018), https://yufeizhao.com/211/
//   - Cambridge Part III Combinatorics (Bollobás 2021–22; Leader),
//     https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/postgrad/part-iii/files/GtC_2021-22/Combinatorics/Combinatorics_Bollobas.pdf
//   Lists of named results:
//   - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_combinatorics_topics
//   - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_theorems (combinatorics / number theory entries)
//
// Gaps filled after the comparison: Kruskal–Katona (both Cambridge courses), Ehrhart polynomials
// (EC1 §4.6), Zeckendorf, Paley/quadratic-residue tournaments (A–S §9.1), FKG, Rado, and a chapter on
// regularity and additive combinatorics (regularity and removal lemmas, Roth, Combinatorial
// Nullstellensatz, Cauchy–Davenport, Erdős–Ginzburg–Ziv, sum–product, Plünnecke–Ruzsa, cap sets).
// Deliberate omissions (covered elsewhere in the atlas or out of scope): matrix-tree theorem and
// electrical networks (circ-2.12ff, stoch ch. 8), Hall/König/Menger and matroids (algo ch. 10–11),
// birthday and coupon-collector problems (algo-12.5, algo-12.6), extremal graph theory beyond Turán
// (graph), block designs and codes (info), hypergeometric/WZ summation (Aigner ch. 4), species,
// Littlewood–Richardson. Topics outside the three references (Ramsey theory beyond Ramsey numbers,
// Kruskal–Katona, Kasteleyn/Aztec/arctic circle, the polynomial method, cap sets, Plünnecke–Ruzsa) keep
// a ref to the nearest related section; read Graham–Rothschild–Spencer, Bollobás’s “Combinatorics”
